= Discussion <discussion>

The TAD of p53#sym.alpha forms two main helical regions in order to bind to interaction partners. These are in TAD1 (residues 19-25, though sometimes also referred to as 17-26) and TAD2 (residues 47-55) @Lee2010, and are known to be transiently formed in the unbound state, which can be observed in @figures:vmd_image_rep1 (TAD1 full), @figures:vmd_image_rep3 (TAD2 missing first coil), and @figures:vmd_image_rep4 (TAD2 first coil). Nascent turns at residues 40-44 can also be observed in all four replicas, though they only stabilized for longer periods in replicas 1 and 2 (@figures:300K_from_600K_simulation_dssp_rep1, @figures:300K_from_600K_simulation_dssp_rep2). Replica 2 also shows a transient helix in TAD1 at residues 28-32 (@figures:vmd_image_rep2), which is not part of the known binding region. This helix was only observed in replica 2, and it is unclear whether this is a sampling artifact or if it has a functional role. p53#sym.alpha with the proline at residue 27 mutated has been shown to have increased helicity in this region, allowing the helix to extend, though this still does not address residues 28-32 forming a helix in the presence of proline @Borcherds2014.
